import type { SicProfile } from '../types.js';

/**
 * Harris County's SIC code → equipment life tables, tax year 2026.
 *
 * Source: Harris Central Appraisal District, "Business & Industrial Personal
 * Property Division — Schedule Value Calculation Guidelines, Tax Year 2026",
 * general personal property pages 6–28 and industrial pages 29–45.
 * https://hcad.org/assets/uploads/pdf/resources/2026/2026-PP-Calc-Guide.pdf
 *
 * Texas does not depreciate machinery on a single life. The district keys it to
 * what the business actually does: at 2022 cost, a $100,000 machine on the
 * eight-year life is worth appreciably less than the same machine on fifteen
 * years, and which one applies is decided by the taxpayer's SIC code rather
 * than by anything about the machine. 559 of the 1,063 codes here sit on eight
 * years and 172 on fifteen, so the ten-year default this replaces was wrong in
 * both directions for most businesses.
 *
 * Generated by `scripts/extract-hcad-sic.py` and committed rather than parsed at
 * runtime, for the same reason as the depreciation schedules: these are
 * published figures that change once a year and decide what a client renders,
 * so a change to them should be reviewable in a diff.
 *
 * The guide prints both tables twice — once alphabetically, once numerically —
 * which gives every row an independent second reading. All 1,063 codes were
 * parsed from both printings and agreed on every field. Five codes are not
 * printed exactly twice and each was checked by hand: 2499A and 3553 are
 * duplicated within a page by the guide itself, 4226P and 4227 are cross-listed
 * in both tables with identical lives, and 7299A is absent from the numerical
 * listing. None is a parse failure.
 *
 * `miscLife` is extracted and carried but deliberately not applied anywhere.
 * The guide's page 1 gives fixed lives for furniture (8) and office equipment
 * (6), and it is not established that the misc column overrides those rather
 * than covering some third thing — so it is here to be looked at, not used.
 */
